{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Serena Williams tennis career began at 1995 but didn't compete in 1996.","title":"Serena Williams's early career"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She continued through the 1997 season and ended in the top 20 in 1998.","title":"Serena Williams's early career"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Williams sisters are two professional American tennis players: Venus Williams (b. 1980), a seven-time Grand Slam title winner (singles), and Serena Williams (b. 1981), twenty-three-time Grand Slam title winner (singles), both of whom were coached from an early age by their parents Richard Williams and Oracene Price.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" There is a noted professional rivalry between them – between the 2001 US Open and the 2017 Australian Open tournaments, they met in nine Grand Slam singles finals.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They became the first two players, female or male, to play in 4 consecutive grand slam singles finals from the 2002 French Open to the 2003 Australian Open; Serena famously won all 4 to complete the first of two "Serena Slams".","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Between 2000 and 2016, a 17-year span, they collectively won 12 Wimbledon singles titles (Venus won 5 and Serena won 7).","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" By winning the 2001 Australian Open women's doubles title, they became the 5th pair to complete the Career Doubles Grand Slam and the only pair to complete the Career Doubles Golden Slam.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" At the time, Venus and Serena were only 20 and 19 years old, respectively.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Since then they have gone on to add another two Olympic gold medals in the 2008 Beijing Olympics and the 2012 London Olympics.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Nearly a decade later, the duo would go on to win 4 consecutive grand slam doubles titles from 2009 Wimbledon through 2010 Roland Garros, which would catapult them to co-No.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" 1 doubles players on 7 June 2010.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" Two weeks later, on 21 June 2010, Serena would hold the No. 1 singles ranking and Venus would be right behind her at No. 2 in singles.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" Their most recent grand slam doubles titles came at the 2012 Wimbledon & 2016 Wimbledon events.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" They remain very close, often watching each other's matches in support, even after one of them has been knocked out of a tournament.","title":"Williams sisters"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt (born 18 March 1971) is a former tennis player from South Africa, who played as a professional from 1988 to 2001.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" She twice represented her native country at the Summer Olympics, in 1992 and 1996, and was a member of the South African Fed Cup Team in 1992 and 1994–1997.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2006, de Swardt became a U.S. citizen.","title":"Mariaan de Swardt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles was the women's doubles event of the hundred-and-thirteenth edition of Wimbledon, the third Grand Slam of the year, the most prestigious tournament on grass courts, and the oldest tennis tournament in the world.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Martina Hingis and Jana Novotná were the defending champions but only Novotná competed that year with Natasha Zvereva.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Novotná and Zvereva lost in the semifinals to Mariaan de Swardt and Elena Tatarkova.","title":"1999 Wimbledon Championships – Women's Doubles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Mariaan de Swardt won in the final 3–6, 7–6, 7–5 against Barbara Schett.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Singles"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lisa Raymond and Rennae Stubbs won in the final 6–4, 6–4 against Mariaan de Swardt and Mary Joe Fernández.","title":"1998 Boston Cup – Doubles"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Serena Williams has far more top‑level achievements – she has won 23 Grand Slam singles titles, multiple Wimbledon singles crowns (seven) and completed a career Grand Slam, whereas Mariaan de Swardt’s record includes a single WTA singles title, a few doubles finals and Olympic appearances but no Grand Slam singles titles.
